{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JDCHWBeuXxqz3JidiAmgDF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n5\t- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n6\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (3 conditions) — Revised per D012\n10\t\n11\t1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. The signal is strongest and most robust at the **type-pair level** (pairwise SCS → confusion), validated across NER (Few-NERD), RE (FewRel), and ED (MAVEN with MiniLM). Per-type aggregation (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas. Evidence required: (a) significant pairwise SCS-confusion correlation on >=2 datasets, (b) extreme-batch accuracy gap on FewRel, (c) >=2 models.\n12\t\n13\t2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n14\t\n15\t3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the pairwise-similarity-thresholded graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). SDGD directly leverages pairwise confusability (validated signal), avoiding per-type aggregation confounds. Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n16\t\n17\t## Story Spine — Revised per D012\n18\t\n19\t- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n20\t\n21\t- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). No prior work isolates confusability from count, or characterizes when aggregated confusability measures lose signal.\n22\t\n23\t- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE across three tasks (NER, RE, ED). We discover: (1) a multi-channel degradation model (count→recall, confusability→type confusion), (2) pairwise type confusability is a robust cross-task signal, (3) per-type aggregation of confusability is confounded by type centrality in homogeneous schemas. We operationalize pairwise confusability into SDGD, a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n24\t\n25\t- **Three-task validation**:\n26\t  - NER (Few-NERD, 66 types, cross-domain): Pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), per-type ρ=-0.293 (p=0.02), 10-28x confusion ratio\n27\t  - RE (FewRel, 80 relations, cross-domain): BCS-controlled Δ=-31.2pp (80-rel formal); count curve acc 85.7%→50.4%; BCS effect scales 3.4x vs 16-rel\n28\t  - ED (MAVEN, 168 event types, same-domain): Pairwise MiniLM r=0.120 (p≈0); per-type positive (centrality confound case study)\n29\t\n30\t- **Headline result template**: \"Pairwise type confusability independently predicts extraction degradation across NER, RE, and ED tasks (r=0.12-0.16, p<1e-6), with 10-28x more type confusion errors in high-confusability batches. On semantically homogeneous schemas, per-type aggregation obscures the signal through centrality confounding, but pairwise confusability remains valid. SDGD-zero, operating on pairwise similarity, improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD.\"\n31\t\n32\t## Core Insight — Multi-Channel Degradation + Pairwise Robustness\n33\t\n34\tSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n35\t- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt → attention dilution → entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n36\t- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types → interference during type assignment → correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n37\t\n38\tThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable.\n39\t\n40\t**Pairwise vs Aggregated Confusability**:\n41\t- **Pairwise confusability** (SCS between type pairs) is robust across all tasks and schema types. Validated on Few-NERD (Qwen3, r=0.156) and MAVEN (MiniLM, r=0.120).\n42\t- **Per-type aggregation** (mean BCS = average pairwise similarity to all other types) introduces a centrality confound: in semantically homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), types with many semantic neighbors are central, well-represented types that LLMs extract well → positive correlation (high mean BCS → high F1), masking the pairwise confusability signal.\n43\t- **SDGD operates at pairwise level**, directly using the validated signal to construct the confusability graph for decomposition. It avoids the aggregation confound entirely.\n44\t\n45\t## Evidence Hierarchy (D012)\n46\t\n47\t### Tier 1: PRIMARY — Pairwise SCS → Confusion\n48\t\n49\tThe cleanest evidence. Directly measures: are semantically similar type pairs more often confused?\n50\t\n51\t**Few-NERD (NER)** — Qwen3 embeddings:\n52\t- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs)\n53\t- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n54\t- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n55\t- Immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n56\t\n57\t**MAVEN (ED)** — MiniLM embeddings:\n58\t- Pearson r=+0.120 (p≈0, n=11026 type pairs)\n59\t- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=+0.191 (p<0.001)\n60\t- Cross-task replication on same-domain schema\n61\t- Note: Qwen3 pairwise not significant on MAVEN (r=-0.007, p=0.41) — embedding choice matters for pairwise signal in homogeneous schemas\n62\t\n63\t### Tier 2: STRONG SECONDARY — FewRel Cross-Task Replication\n64\t\n65\tLarge effect size, cross-task (RE), scaling with schema complexity:\n66\t\n67\t**Formal 80-rel study** (exp_fewrel_formal_80rel):\n68\t- Count curve: acc 85.7% (count=1) → 50.4% (count=80), BCS 0→0.97\n69\t- BCS-controlled: count_8 low-BCS 82.3% vs high-BCS 51.0% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n70\t- BCS-controlled: count_16 low-BCS 82.8% vs high-BCS 51.6% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n71\t- **Scaling**: vs 16-rel, degradation 2.8x (35.3pp vs 12.6pp), BCS effect 3.4x (-31.2pp vs -9.1pp)\n72\t\n73\t**16-rel sanity check** (exp_fewrel_formal):\n74\t- count_8: low-BCS 88.9% vs high-BCS 79.8% (Δ=-9.1pp)\n75\t\n76\t**Pilot extreme batches** (fewrel_pilot, MiniLM):\n77\t- count_4: Δ=-20.9pp, count_8: Δ=-50.0pp, count_16: Δ=-48.6pp\n78\t\n79\tKey insight: confusability effect amplifies with schema size — direct evidence for multi-channel degradation scaling\n80\t\n81\t### Tier 3: SUPPORTING — Few-NERD Per-type Analysis\n82\t\n83\tBridges pairwise and batch-level, demonstrates per-type signal in cross-domain schema:\n84\t- Spearman ρ=-0.293 (p=0.020, n=63 types, freq≥5) — Qwen3 embeddings\n85\t- Partial r=-0.200 (p=0.116) controlling for log-frequency\n86\t- Quartile comparison: high-BCS types F1=0.228 vs low-BCS F1=0.310 (Δ=-8.2pp)\n87\t- BCS-frequency uncorrelated at type level (r=0.042, p=0.74)\n88\t- MiniLM per-type: ρ≈0 (p=0.97) — extraction model alignment required for per-type signal\n89\t\n90\t### Tier 4: DISCUSSION — MAVEN Per-type Centrality Confound\n91\t\n92\tMethodological case study on when aggregated confusability measures fail:\n93\t- Qwen3 per-type: ρ=+0.189 (p=0.021) — OPPOSITE direction\n94\t- MiniLM per-type: ρ=+0.208 (p=0.011) — ALSO positive, ruling out embedding compression as sole explanation\n95\t- Both embeddings agree: positive direction on MAVEN is an intrinsic property of the type structure\n96\t- Explanation: in homogeneous schemas, mean BCS conflates confusability with type centrality (types with many semantic neighbors → common/central types → higher F1)\n97\t- This is stronger than \"domain boundary\" — it identifies the specific mechanism (aggregation confound) rather than just the boundary condition\n98\t\n99\t### Tier 5: MECHANISTIC — Error Taxonomy\n100\t\n101\tConfusion rate differential demonstrates the mechanism:\n102\t- Extreme batches: high-BCS 10-28x more type confusion errors vs low-BCS\n103\t- Random batches: 1.7-2.4x ratio (still significant, smaller effect)\n104\t- Type confusion errors increase with count AND with confusability (additive)\n105\t\n106\t## Embedding Ablation (D010)\n107\t\n108\t2×3 matrix: {Qwen3, MiniLM} × {Few-NERD, FewRel, MAVEN}\n109\t\n110\t### Per-type (Layer 2)\n111\t| | Few-NERD | FewRel | MAVEN |\n112\t|---|---|---|---|\n113\t| Qwen3 | ρ=-0.293 ✓ | pending | ρ=+0.189 ✗ |\n114\t| MiniLM | ρ≈0 ✗ | Δ=21-50pp ✓ | ρ=+0.208 ✗ |\n115\t\n116\t### Pairwise (Layer 1)\n117\t| | Few-NERD | MAVEN |\n118\t|---|---|---|\n119\t| Qwen3 | r=0.156 ✓ | r=-0.007 ✗ |\n120\t| MiniLM | — | r=+0.120 ✓ |\n121\t\n122\t**Interpretation**: No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings (Qwen3) capture per-type confusability in cross-domain schemas. Generic embeddings (MiniLM) capture pairwise confusability in same-domain schemas. SDGD should use task-appropriate embeddings; SDGD-cal (empirical confusion) bypasses embedding choice entirely.\n123\t\n124\t## Venue Fit Risks\n125\t\n126\t- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024).\n127\t- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios — clinical, regulatory, multilingual.\n128\t- **Multi-model generalizability**: Qwen3-32B primary + LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012).\n129\t- **MAVEN doesn't support hypothesis?**: Pairwise MiniLM does support it. Per-type centrality confound is methodological contribution, not failure.\n130\t- **Embedding sensitivity**: 2×3 ablation matrix is itself a contribution — characterizes when which proxy works.\n131\t\n132\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n133\t\n134\t| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n135\t|---------|------------------|------------|\n136\t| Only one model | LLaMA-3.3-70B replication | exp-012 |\n137\t| Cross-task replication | Pairwise: Few-NERD ✓ + MAVEN MiniLM ✓. Batch: FewRel ✓. Three tasks. | exp-002, exp-004, fewrel |\n138\t| No practical method | SDGD framework (-zero, -cal) | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n139\t| Confusability measure validity | Embedding ablation (D010) + pairwise vs per-type analysis | D010, exp-015 |\n140\t| Effect size small | Pairwise r=0.12-0.16 + FewRel Δ=21-50pp + confusion ratio 10-28x | multiple |\n141\t| MAVEN per-type opposite | Centrality confound analysis; pairwise still works | MAVEN MiniLM |\n142\t| Embedding choice matters? | 2×3 ablation table; SDGD-cal bypasses embedding entirely | D010 |\n143\t| Why not per-type splitting? | SDGD vs per-type vs random ablation | exp-013, exp-014 |\n144\t| Latency overhead | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n145\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf5393c5-ac4b-4e08-b546-c8efba504dc2","uuid":"f71cd0d1-d237-4e3c-85a4-d387d991ae0d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:33:07.439Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n\n## Core Claims (3 conditions) — Revised per D012\n\n1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. The signal is strongest and most robust at the **type-pair level** (pairwise SCS → confusion), validated across NER (Few-NERD), RE (FewRel), and ED (MAVEN with MiniLM). Per-type aggregation (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas. Evidence required: (a) significant pairwise SCS-confusion correlation on >=2 datasets, (b) extreme-batch accuracy gap on FewRel, (c) >=2 models.\n\n2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n\n3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the pairwise-similarity-thresholded graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). SDGD directly leverages pairwise confusability (validated signal), avoiding per-type aggregation confounds. Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n\n## Story Spine — Revised per D012\n\n- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). No prior work isolates confusability from count, or characterizes when aggregated confusability measures lose signal.\n\n- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE across three tasks (NER, RE, ED). We discover: (1) a multi-channel degradation model (count→recall, confusability→type confusion), (2) pairwise type confusability is a robust cross-task signal, (3) per-type aggregation of confusability is confounded by type centrality in homogeneous schemas. We operationalize pairwise confusability into SDGD, a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n\n- **Three-task validation**:\n  - NER (Few-NERD, 66 types, cross-domain): Pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), per-type ρ=-0.293 (p=0.02), 10-28x confusion ratio\n  - RE (FewRel, 80 relations, cross-domain): BCS-controlled Δ=-31.2pp (80-rel formal); count curve acc 85.7%→50.4%; BCS effect scales 3.4x vs 16-rel\n  - ED (MAVEN, 168 event types, same-domain): Pairwise MiniLM r=0.120 (p≈0); per-type positive (centrality confound case study)\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"Pairwise type confusability independently predicts extraction degradation across NER, RE, and ED tasks (r=0.12-0.16, p<1e-6), with 10-28x more type confusion errors in high-confusability batches. On semantically homogeneous schemas, per-type aggregation obscures the signal through centrality confounding, but pairwise confusability remains valid. SDGD-zero, operating on pairwise similarity, improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD.\"\n\n## Core Insight — Multi-Channel Degradation + Pairwise Robustness\n\nSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt → attention dilution → entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types → interference during type assignment → correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n\nThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable.\n\n**Pairwise vs Aggregated Confusability**:\n- **Pairwise confusability** (SCS between type pairs) is robust across all tasks and schema types. Validated on Few-NERD (Qwen3, r=0.156) and MAVEN (MiniLM, r=0.120).\n- **Per-type aggregation** (mean BCS = average pairwise similarity to all other types) introduces a centrality confound: in semantically homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), types with many semantic neighbors are central, well-represented types that LLMs extract well → positive correlation (high mean BCS → high F1), masking the pairwise confusability signal.\n- **SDGD operates at pairwise level**, directly using the validated signal to construct the confusability graph for decomposition. It avoids the aggregation confound entirely.\n\n## Evidence Hierarchy (D012)\n\n### Tier 1: PRIMARY — Pairwise SCS → Confusion\n\nThe cleanest evidence. Directly measures: are semantically similar type pairs more often confused?\n\n**Few-NERD (NER)** — Qwen3 embeddings:\n- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs)\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n- Immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n\n**MAVEN (ED)** — MiniLM embeddings:\n- Pearson r=+0.120 (p≈0, n=11026 type pairs)\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=+0.191 (p<0.001)\n- Cross-task replication on same-domain schema\n- Note: Qwen3 pairwise not significant on MAVEN (r=-0.007, p=0.41) — embedding choice matters for pairwise signal in homogeneous schemas\n\n### Tier 2: STRONG SECONDARY — FewRel Cross-Task Replication\n\nLarge effect size, cross-task (RE), scaling with schema complexity:\n\n**Formal 80-rel study** (exp_fewrel_formal_80rel):\n- Count curve: acc 85.7% (count=1) → 50.4% (count=80), BCS 0→0.97\n- BCS-controlled: count_8 low-BCS 82.3% vs high-BCS 51.0% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n- BCS-controlled: count_16 low-BCS 82.8% vs high-BCS 51.6% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n- **Scaling**: vs 16-rel, degradation 2.8x (35.3pp vs 12.6pp), BCS effect 3.4x (-31.2pp vs -9.1pp)\n\n**16-rel sanity check** (exp_fewrel_formal):\n- count_8: low-BCS 88.9% vs high-BCS 79.8% (Δ=-9.1pp)\n\n**Pilot extreme batches** (fewrel_pilot, MiniLM):\n- count_4: Δ=-20.9pp, count_8: Δ=-50.0pp, count_16: Δ=-48.6pp\n\nKey insight: confusability effect amplifies with schema size — direct evidence for multi-channel degradation scaling\n\n### Tier 3: SUPPORTING — Few-NERD Per-type Analysis\n\nBridges pairwise and batch-level, demonstrates per-type signal in cross-domain schema:\n- Spearman ρ=-0.293 (p=0.020, n=63 types, freq≥5) — Qwen3 embeddings\n- Partial r=-0.200 (p=0.116) controlling for log-frequency\n- Quartile comparison: high-BCS types F1=0.228 vs low-BCS F1=0.310 (Δ=-8.2pp)\n- BCS-frequency uncorrelated at type level (r=0.042, p=0.74)\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ≈0 (p=0.97) — extraction model alignment required for per-type signal\n\n### Tier 4: DISCUSSION — MAVEN Per-type Centrality Confound\n\nMethodological case study on when aggregated confusability measures fail:\n- Qwen3 per-type: ρ=+0.189 (p=0.021) — OPPOSITE direction\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ=+0.208 (p=0.011) — ALSO positive, ruling out embedding compression as sole explanation\n- Both embeddings agree: positive direction on MAVEN is an intrinsic property of the type structure\n- Explanation: in homogeneous schemas, mean BCS conflates confusability with type centrality (types with many semantic neighbors → common/central types → higher F1)\n- This is stronger than \"domain boundary\" — it identifies the specific mechanism (aggregation confound) rather than just the boundary condition\n\n### Tier 5: MECHANISTIC — Error Taxonomy\n\nConfusion rate differential demonstrates the mechanism:\n- Extreme batches: high-BCS 10-28x more type confusion errors vs low-BCS\n- Random batches: 1.7-2.4x ratio (still significant, smaller effect)\n- Type confusion errors increase with count AND with confusability (additive)\n\n## Embedding Ablation (D010)\n\n2×3 matrix: {Qwen3, MiniLM} × {Few-NERD, FewRel, MAVEN}\n\n### Per-type (Layer 2)\n| | Few-NERD | FewRel | MAVEN |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen3 | ρ=-0.293 ✓ | pending | ρ=+0.189 ✗ |\n| MiniLM | ρ≈0 ✗ | Δ=21-50pp ✓ | ρ=+0.208 ✗ |\n\n### Pairwise (Layer 1)\n| | Few-NERD | MAVEN |\n|---|---|---|\n| Qwen3 | r=0.156 ✓ | r=-0.007 ✗ |\n| MiniLM | — | r=+0.120 ✓ |\n\n**Interpretation**: No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings (Qwen3) capture per-type confusability in cross-domain schemas. Generic embeddings (MiniLM) capture pairwise confusability in same-domain schemas. SDGD should use task-appropriate embeddings; SDGD-cal (empirical confusion) bypasses embedding choice entirely.\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024).\n- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios — clinical, regulatory, multilingual.\n- **Multi-model generalizability**: Qwen3-32B primary + LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012).\n- **MAVEN doesn't support hypothesis?**: Pairwise MiniLM does support it. Per-type centrality confound is methodological contribution, not failure.\n- **Embedding sensitivity**: 2×3 ablation matrix is itself a contribution — characterizes when which proxy works.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n|---------|------------------|------------|\n| Only one model | LLaMA-3.3-70B replication | exp-012 |\n| Cross-task replication | Pairwise: Few-NERD ✓ + MAVEN MiniLM ✓. Batch: FewRel ✓. Three tasks. | exp-002, exp-004, fewrel |\n| No practical method | SDGD framework (-zero, -cal) | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n| Confusability measure validity | Embedding ablation (D010) + pairwise vs per-type analysis | D010, exp-015 |\n| Effect size small | Pairwise r=0.12-0.16 + FewRel Δ=21-50pp + confusion ratio 10-28x | multiple |\n| MAVEN per-type opposite | Centrality confound analysis; pairwise still works | MAVEN MiniLM |\n| Embedding choice matters? | 2×3 ablation table; SDGD-cal bypasses embedding entirely | D010 |\n| Why not per-type splitting? | SDGD vs per-type vs random ablation | exp-013, exp-014 |\n| Latency overhead | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n","numLines":145,"startLine":1,"totalLines":145}}}
